---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import demo from '../data/demo.json'
import tool from '../data/tool_results.json'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const f = demo.fingerprint
---

<Base
  title="How it works — CLOISTER"
  description="The four layers in detail: many-to-one ToUnicode chaff, posture-gated post-quantum decryption, paired-gap Tardos fingerprinting, and zero-knowledge authorship provenance."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pt-14 pb-10">
    <h1
      class="text-[2.1rem] sm:text-[2.7rem] leading-[1.1] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-slate-300"
    >
      How it works
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      The organising idea is that “stop AI reading my document” is not one problem. It is
      four, they have different adversaries, and a mechanism that addresses one of them is
      often useless against the others. Each layer below states what it assumes, what it
      buys, and where it stops.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================= layer D -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 mt-6">
    <div class="rule mb-8"></div>
    <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
      <span
        class="font-mono text-[11px] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-400/12 text-amber-400 tracking-wider"
        >D</span
      >
      <h2 class="text-[22px] font-medium text-slate-300">The chaff layer</h2>
      <span class="text-[13px] text-slate-400">needs nothing installed anywhere</span>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      A PDF contains two independent things: instructions for painting glyphs, and a table
      claiming which characters those glyphs represent. Nothing enforces agreement between
      them. A human reads the first. Every extraction library, RAG loader and agent file
      reader reads the second.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      So the painted glyphs are your document, and the character table describes fluent,
      innocuous prose about facilities maintenance. An automated pipeline does not error —
      it terminates confidently on the wrong document, with no signal that anything
      happened.
    </p>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-6 mt-6">
      <h3 class="text-[13.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">
        The detail that makes the decoy readable
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2.5 text-[13.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        A first attempt maps each drawn glyph to one decoy character. That produces word
        salad, which any filter would flag. The fix came from the PDF specification: a
        <span class="font-mono text-slate-300">ToUnicode</span> <span class="font-mono text-slate-300">bfchar</span>
        entry may map a single glyph ID to a <em>string</em>, not a character. So six drawn
        glyphs can report forty-three characters of grammatical English.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[13.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        That removed the need for a constrained-decoding language model to generate
        length-matched decoy text — the decoy no longer has to match the true text
        word-for-word in length at all. Verified with six glyphs spelling
        <span class="font-mono text-slate-300">SECRET</span> on the page while all three
        extractors reported a fluent sentence, and OCR read
        <span class="font-mono text-slate-300">SECRET</span>.
      </p>
      <dl class="mt-5 grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-[12.5px]">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-slate-400">Terms recovered by extraction</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-emerald-400 text-[15px] mt-0.5">
            {demo.headline.text_layer_recovered}/{demo.headline.terms_total}
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-slate-400">Terms recovered by OCR</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-rose-400 text-[15px] mt-0.5">
            {demo.headline.ocr_recovered}/{demo.headline.terms_total}
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-slate-400">Decoy compressibility</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-slate-300 text-[15px] mt-0.5">English-like</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[12.5px] text-slate-400">
        Compressibility matters because it is the cheap test a filter would apply: chaff
        that compressed like random letters would announce itself. <a
          href={`${base}/demo`}
          class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">See both channels live →</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-5 mt-4 border-l-2 border-l-rose-400/70">
      <p class="text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        <strong class="text-slate-300">Where it stops.</strong> OCR and vision models read
        the rendered page. This layer removes the silent, high-volume path and imposes a
        rendering-and-OCR cost — roughly an order of magnitude in tokens — on anyone who
        wants the document anyway. It also breaks screen readers by design, so the tool
        emits a true-text accessible variant and the honest deployment says so on the
        document's face.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================= layer P1 -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 mt-16">
    <div class="rule mb-8"></div>
    <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
      <span
        class="font-mono text-[11px] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-400/12 text-amber-400 tracking-wider"
        >P1 · P2</span
      >
      <h2 class="text-[22px] font-medium text-slate-300">
        The envelope, and the membrane
      </h2>
      <span class="text-[13px] text-slate-400">when the machine is yours to control</span>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      Rights management normally binds a key to an <em>identity</em>. That fails in the
      obvious way: the authorised reader is the one who leaks it, and revoking their access
      afterwards is meaningless because they already hold a usable key. So the key is bound
      to a <em>runtime posture</em> instead.
    </p>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-6 mt-6 font-mono text-[12px] leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto">
      <div class="text-slate-400">ss = HKDF-SHA384(</div>
      <div class="text-slate-300 pl-4">
        ss_X25519 ‖ ss_MLKEM768 ‖ ct_X25519 ‖ ct_MLKEM768 ‖ <span class="text-amber-400"
          >H(policy)</span
        >
      </div>
      <div class="text-slate-400">)</div>
      <div class="text-slate-400 mt-2">
        KW = AES-256-GCM(ss) wraps the content key, AAD = <span class="text-amber-400"
          >H(policy)</span
        >
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      The policy digest is inside the key-derivation transcript, which makes it
      load-bearing rather than advisory. Editing the policy does not produce a permissive
      envelope — it produces a key that no longer reproduces. That holds even against an
      adversary who <em>also holds the signing key</em>, which is exactly where a
      signed-policy scheme fails, and there is a test for that adversary specifically.
    </p>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-6 mt-5">
      <h3 class="text-[13.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">Ordering is the design</h3>
      <p class="mt-2.5 text-[13.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        A sandbox applied <em>after</em> decryption protects nothing, because the plaintext
        already exists in a process that could have leaked it. So the reader enters an
        irreversible runtime first — empty network namespace, a seccomp-BPF filter denying
        16 egress and memory-inspection syscalls, <span class="font-mono">no_new_privs</span>,
        Landlock where the kernel exposes it — and only then presents that posture to obtain
        a short lease.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[13.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        The test for this counts KEM decapsulations during a denied open. The answer has to
        be zero, and it is: the gate runs before any key material exists, not merely before
        the plaintext is returned. The filter also survives
        <span class="font-mono">execve</span>, which is what makes it cover the real
        renderer and any agent runtime that attaches itself downstream.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        Post-quantum matters here specifically because an envelope is a self-contained
        ciphertext an adversary can simply keep. Privileged legal material and embargoed
        research have confidentiality horizons measured in decades — the textbook
        harvest-now-decrypt-later target.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-5 mt-4 border-l-2 border-l-rose-400/70">
      <p class="text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        <strong class="text-slate-300">Where it stops.</strong> Capture exclusion,
        clipboard gating and renderer attestation are not implemented — they are
        per-platform windowing and measurement calls, and on bare X11 the first does not
        exist at all — so the runtime reports them <span class="font-mono">False</span> and
        a policy requiring them fails closed rather than optimistically passing. The device
        keys are ordinary keys in memory; in deployment they would be non-exportable and
        sealed to a TPM or Secure Enclave, which is the one property this code cannot
        demonstrate on its own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================= layer T -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 mt-16">
    <div class="rule mb-8"></div>
    <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
      <span
        class="font-mono text-[11px] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-400/12 text-amber-400 tracking-wider"
        >T</span
      >
      <h2 class="text-[22px] font-medium text-slate-300">The fingerprint</h2>
      <span class="text-[13px] text-slate-400">assumes the leak will happen</span>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      Prevention has a ceiling, so the last layer accepts the leak and makes it
      attributable. Every recipient's copy has inter-word gaps displaced by
      {' '}{f.delta_pt} pt — {f.delta_px_at_300dpi} px at 300 dpi — encoding a
      collusion-resistant Tardos codeword.
    </p>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-6">
      <div class="panel rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="text-[13.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">
          Gaps move in pairs that sum to zero
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
          A one is <span class="font-mono">(base+d, base−d)</span>, a zero the reverse. Line
          length is therefore invariant, displacement cannot accumulate along a line, and
          the right margin never moves. Detection compares two adjacent gaps, which cancels
          global scale error from printing or photographing for free.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="text-[13.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">
          The detector never segments words
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
          It holds the master, so it computes each word's expected window analytically from
          the layout map. Two earlier detectors that found words by ink connectivity both
          scored <em>at chance</em>: one split or merged glyph shifts every following gap
          index and inverts the bits after it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-6 mt-4">
      <h3 class="text-[13.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">
        The accusation test, and why it is two-sided
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2.5 text-[13.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        A coalition that emits the minority bit everywhere produces a
        <em>negatively</em> correlated sequence. The textbook one-sided test names
        <span class="font-mono">0.000</span> of five colluders against that attack — not
        degraded detection, zero detection. Scoring on
        <span class="font-mono">|S|</span> recovers 3.5 of 5 with no false accusations.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[13.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        The threshold is calibrated against each code's own simulated null distribution
        rather than taken as a number of sigma, because at the observation counts a single
        photographed page provides the score is not reliably Gaussian. Codes are also
        screened at generation time: two codes with identical parameters differed by an
        order of magnitude in detection power.
      </p>
      <dl class="mt-5 grid sm:grid-cols-4 gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-[12.5px]">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-slate-400">Traced from one page</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-emerald-400 text-[15px] mt-0.5">
            {tool.tracing_from_one_photographed_page.correct}/{tool
              .tracing_from_one_photographed_page.trials}
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-slate-400">False accusations</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-emerald-400 text-[15px] mt-0.5">
            {tool.tracing_from_one_photographed_page.false_accusations}
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-slate-400">Worst margin</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-slate-300 text-[15px] mt-0.5">
            {tool.tracing_from_one_photographed_page.margin_over_nearest_innocent.min}×
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-slate-400">Codeword coverage</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-slate-300 text-[15px] mt-0.5">
            {tool.scale_test.codeword_coverage}×
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-5 mt-4 border-l-2 border-l-rose-400/70">
      <p class="text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        <strong class="text-slate-300">Where it stops.</strong> Retyping destroys the
        spacing channel. One page traces a single leaker, not a coalition — resisting five
        colluders needs a code an order of magnitude longer and correspondingly more
        recovered material. And the detector has a measured floor that is
        <em>lighting</em>, not resolution: a dim, unevenly lit capture that is also heavily
        downscaled falls to chance. At chance it names nobody rather than naming an
        innocent, and there is a test asserting exactly that.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================= layer A -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 mt-16">
    <div class="rule mb-8"></div>
    <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
      <span
        class="font-mono text-[11px] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-400/12 text-amber-400 tracking-wider"
        >A</span
      >
      <h2 class="text-[22px] font-medium text-slate-300">Authorship provenance</h2>
      <span class="text-[13px] text-slate-400">the academic case, inverted</span>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      The other half of the academic problem is not distribution — it is a submission
      someone did not write. Detection is the wrong primitive for it: the signal is weak,
      the cost of a false positive is an accused student, and the bias falls on non-native
      English writers. So rather than proving a student <em>did</em> cheat, a student can
      offer positive evidence that they wrote it, and nobody runs a classifier over their
      prose.
    </p>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-6">
      <div class="panel rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="text-[13.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">Protecting the student</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
          A keystroke log is surveillance and no institution should hold one. The trail
          never leaves the author's device. What is disclosed is a set of zero-knowledge
          predicates over it — “no single insertion exceeded 400 characters” — via Pedersen
          commitments and bit-decomposition range proofs. Of 861 edit events, zero
          timestamps and zero statistic values appear anywhere in the credential.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="text-[13.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">
          Protecting the institution
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
          Zero-knowledge proofs supply no integrity by themselves — an author could commit
          to whatever statistics suit them. So an attested editor computes the statistics,
          commits to them, and signs the commitments alongside the trail root and the
          submission hash. The proofs are privacy; the signature is integrity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-6 mt-4">
      <h3 class="text-[13.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">
        Five simulated sessions, all producing the same length of text
      </h3>
      <table class="mt-4 w-full text-[13px]">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-ink-700/50">
          {
            [
              ['honest, deliberate (65 min)', 'credential complete', 'good'],
              ['honest, fluent (3 min)', 'credential complete', 'good'],
              ['one bulk paste', 'incomplete — all three predicates fail', 'bad'],
              ['pasted in twelve chunks', 'incomplete — all three predicates fail', 'bad'],
              ['model output retyped by hand', 'credential complete', 'warn'],
            ].map(([s, v, tone]) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="py-2.5 pr-6 text-slate-400">{s}</td>
                <td
                  class={`py-2.5 ${
                    tone === 'good'
                      ? 'text-emerald-400'
                      : tone === 'bad'
                        ? 'text-slate-300'
                        : 'text-rose-400'
                  }`}
                >
                  {v}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        That last row is the fundamental limit, and the suite asserts it rather than hiding
        it. This layer attests a <em>process</em>, not an <em>origin</em>: someone who reads
        model output off a phone and types it in at human pace satisfies every predicate.
        What changes is that the shortcut is no longer free, and the honest student is no
        longer at the mercy of a classifier.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-5 mt-4 border-l-2 border-l-amber-400/70">
      <p class="text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        <strong class="text-slate-300">The default policy has no time predicate, on
        purpose.</strong> A wall-clock floor looks like an obvious check and is a bad one.
        Two honest sessions producing identical output took 65 minutes and 3 minutes; a
        30-minute floor passes the first and fails the second. A floor penalises fluency and
        rewards leaving the editor open. Rhythm statistics are worse — they misfire on
        dictation, on motor impairment, and on drafting in a second language.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 mt-16">
    <div class="rule mb-8"></div>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a
        href={`${base}/evidence`}
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded bg-amber-400 px-4 py-2.5 text-[13.5px] font-medium text-ink-950 hover:bg-amber-300 transition-colors"
        >Every claim and its test →</a
      >
      <a
        href={`${base}/limits`}
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded border border-ink-600/80 px-4 py-2.5 text-[13.5px] text-slate-300 hover:border-amber-400/60 hover:text-amber-400 transition-colors"
        >What it cannot do</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
